Material Symbols — SVG path subsets for `icons`
================================================================================

Included files
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/material_symbols/*.svg                 (the vendored upstream outlines, one per icon)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/material_symbols/LICENSE               (the upstream licence copy)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/material_symbols/upstream_codepoints.txt (the upstream name/codepoint list)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/material_symbols/UPSTREAM_HAS_NO_NOTICE (a probe result, see below)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/src/data.rs        (the generated SVG path table)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/src/fallback.rs    (the generated fallback geometry)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/src/names.rs       (the generated `IconName` table)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/src/upstream_aliases.rs (the generated same-picture relation)
    crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/icon_catalog.txt (the generated declaration list)

Origin
    Material Symbols, by Google LLC.
    Project:  https://github.com/google/material-design-icons
    Pin:      commit bd8cb85bd4bad964fe6918f79665bb40c3a8efef (master @ 2026-09-25)
    Style:    symbols/web/<name>/materialsymbolsoutlined/<name>_24px.svg
    Names:    variablefont/MaterialSymbolsOutlined[FILL,GRAD,opsz,wght].codepoints
    SHA-256 of the upstream LICENSE at the pinned commit:
        58d1e17ffe5109a7ae296caafcadfdbe6a7d176f0bc4ab01e12a689b0499d8bd
    SHA-256 of the upstream codepoint list at the pinned commit:
        225bd09137103cb7746bc93dc08d08764c9f0c3bd04f4b958d4a3c3c19432dd6

    The files are vendored, never hand-fetched. Refresh with:
        python3 crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/vendor_material_symbols.py --refresh

What it is
    The **SVG path data** (`d`) of one outline per icon this crate names — 4323 icons: the 75
    tokens the crate declares by hand plus the whole 4248-name Material Symbols catalogue — not
    the icon font, and not a rendering of it. Each `d` is copied **verbatim**: no outline was
    redrawn, simplified, re-encoded or otherwise altered, so the shipped geometry is upstream's
    to the byte. `crates/rust-widgets-icons/src/data.rs` is generated from the vendored files and
    is never hand-edited:
        python3 crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/gen_icon_data.py            # write the table
        python3 crates/rust-widgets-icons/tools/gen_icon_data.py --check    # verify it is current

    One revision exception, recorded in `vendor_material_symbols.RELEASE_TREE_SOURCES` and
    checked by the generator: 146 names do not come from the pinned commit. 73 of them
    (`RELEASE_ONLY`) are absent from it — the retired Material Icons spellings such as `place`,
    `people` and `help_outline`, which a host may well pass to `Icon::set_icon` — and 73
    (`LEGACY_GRID`) exist there only in upstream's legacy 24-unit, y-down encoding, which this
    crate's single design grid cannot store without re-encoding the numbers. Both groups are
    vendored from the Material Symbols release tree (fonts.gstatic.com) instead, which publishes
    every name on the same 960-unit grid. Their outlines are therefore the current release's,
    not the pinned commit's; no other entry has that caveat.

    The data is behind the `icons` feature. `desktop`, `tablet` and `mobile` enable it, so the
    device profiles with a reason to want real outlines get them; `mini` and `embedded` do not,
    because those are sized builds where a payload the caller did not ask for is wrong. A build
    without the feature draws `crates/rust-widgets-icons/src/fallback.rs`, which carries a
    coarse flattening of the **same** `d` strings for the tokens this crate's own controls name
    directly — derived, not copied — so the fallback and the data cannot describe different
    shapes for one icon. A catalogue token in such a build has no fallback and reports that it
    has no geometry rather than drawing a wrong picture; duplicating 4248 flattened outlines
    there would add roughly 12 MB of constants to every `mini`/`embedded` binary.

